Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.90,0:00:03.86,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}This glass lens is about nine microns thick.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:03.90,0:00:06.74,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}This patterned plate is just one micron thick.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.81,0:00:08.19,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Shine green light through both,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:08.23,0:00:11.25,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and they focus it to exactly the same spot.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:11.32,0:00:14.74,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}But in white light, the glass lens still makes one sharp focus,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:14.78,0:00:18.05,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}while the plate smears the colours along its axis.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.12,0:00:22.48,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}How can one micron of glass do a thick lens's job? And what's the catch?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:23.30,0:00:25.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Inside glass, light slows down to c/n,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:25.82,0:00:29.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}where n, the refractive index, is about 1.5.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:30.03,0:00:33.12,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Follow two paths, one through a block of glass of height h,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.16,0:00:37.54,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}one through air. In the glass, the crests bunch up and fall behind.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:37.61,0:00:41.76,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The glass path lags by (n − 1)h:
Dialogue: 0,0:00:41.80,0:00:48.54,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}a phase delay φ = k₀(n − 1)h, where k₀ = 2π/λ.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:48.61,0:00:52.73,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}If the glass is thin, each point is simply delayed on its own:
Dialogue: 0,0:00:52.77,0:00:57.38,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}the outgoing field is the incoming one, times e^(iφ(x)).
Dialogue: 0,0:00:57.45,0:00:58.92,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}That's the thin element approximation,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:58.96,0:01:02.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}TEA. Make the glass thickest in the middle,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:02.32,0:01:06.34,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and a flat wave leaves curved, converging to a focus.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:06.41,0:01:09.29,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}It's the simplest, fastest rung on a ladder of models.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:09.33,0:01:13.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Remember its one assumption: every point on its own.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:13.88,0:01:18.85,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Now the key trick. Phase is an angle, so a delay of one full turn,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:18.89,0:01:22.63,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}2π, changes nothing: every crest lands where the next one was.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:22.70,0:01:26.50,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}In glass, one full turn of delay takes a height of λ/(n − 1).
Dialogue: 0,0:01:26.54,0:01:30.86,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}For green light, that's about 1.1 microns.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:30.93,0:01:34.11,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}So slice the lens into slabs of exactly that height.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:34.18,0:01:36.30,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Each full slab only adds whole turns.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:36.34,0:01:38.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}It changes nothing, so throw it away.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:39.03,0:01:42.51,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Keep just the curved remainders, and let them drop onto the base.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:43.48,0:01:45.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}What's left is a kinoform:
Dialogue: 0,0:01:45.46,0:01:48.85,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}rings called Fresnel zones, about one micron thick.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:48.92,0:01:52.84,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The zones narrow toward the edge, where light must bend most steeply.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:52.88,0:01:55.59,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}There, they're only a few wavelengths wide.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:55.66,0:01:58.02,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}At the design wavelength, the kinoform
Dialogue: 0,0:01:58.06,0:02:01.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}multiplies the light by exactly the same e^(iφ).
Dialogue: 0,0:02:01.74,0:02:05.24,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}So it focuses to exactly the same spot.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:06.06,0:02:09.60,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Real fabrication often makes just N discrete heights,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:09.64,0:02:11.68,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}so each zone becomes a staircase.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:11.75,0:02:14.73,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Each zone works like a tooth of a grating.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:14.77,0:02:17.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}A perfect ramp sends all the light into one diffraction order:
Dialogue: 0,0:02:17.82,0:02:19.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}the one aimed at the focus.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:19.79,0:02:23.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Steps leak light into other orders, and that light misses the focus.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:23.85,0:02:27.41,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}How much? At each point, draw an arrow for the gap between its
Dialogue: 0,0:02:27.45,0:02:31.12,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}phase and the perfect ramp, then add the arrows head to tail.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:31.19,0:02:32.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}For the ramp, they all line up.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:32.82,0:02:36.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}But each step holds its phase while the ramp keeps turning,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:36.12,0:02:39.66,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}so the chain curls, and its end-to-end arrow is shorter.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:40.33,0:02:44.76,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Its squared length is the share of light in the focus: η = sinc²(1/N).
Dialogue: 0,0:02:44.80,0:02:53.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}That's 41% for two levels, 81% for four, 95% for eight, 99% for sixteen.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:54.22,0:02:57.14,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Now colour. A kinoform works by diffraction:
Dialogue: 0,0:02:57.18,0:03:01.45,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}its zones are fixed, and longer wavelengths bend more at each one.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:01.52,0:03:05.17,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}So its focal length depends on colour,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:05.21,0:03:10.53,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}f = f₀λ₀/λ: red focuses closest, and blue farthest.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:10.60,0:03:13.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}A glass lens does the opposite, and far more gently:
Dialogue: 0,0:03:13.44,0:03:17.98,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}glass bends blue slightly more, so blue focuses a little closer.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:18.05,0:03:20.67,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Opticians score this with the Abbe number:
Dialogue: 0,0:03:20.71,0:03:26.87,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}about +60 for common glass, but −3.45 for every diffractive lens.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:26.94,0:03:28.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Since the signs are opposite,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:28.20,0:03:32.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}a weak diffractive surface can cancel a glass lens's colour error.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:32.74,0:03:35.82,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Or software can correct the colours afterwards.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:36.64,0:03:39.60,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Now the deepest catch. The thin element approximation
Dialogue: 0,0:03:39.64,0:03:43.98,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}assumes every column of glass acts on its own. Is that true?
Dialogue: 0,0:03:44.05,0:03:48.55,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Zoom in on zones four wavelengths wide, with walls two wavelengths tall.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:49.22,0:03:52.75,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Solve Maxwell's equations directly, and the clean picture breaks.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:52.79,0:03:56.25,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Light crowds along the walls, shoots out in jets,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:56.29,0:03:58.24,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and leaves shadows beside them.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:58.31,0:04:03.01,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}So less light reaches the focus: TEA promises all of it,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:03.05,0:04:06.09,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}but the full solution delivers about 70%.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:06.16,0:04:08.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The narrower the zones, the bigger the gap.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:08.60,0:04:11.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Only zones many wavelengths wide are safe.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:11.49,0:04:14.31,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Two ways out. Metasurfaces build the phase
Dialogue: 0,0:04:14.35,0:04:16.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}from pillars narrower than a wavelength,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:16.46,0:04:19.79,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}each one's delay computed from the full equations.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:19.86,0:04:22.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Or drop the shortcut, and simulate the whole
Dialogue: 0,0:04:22.22,0:04:25.75,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}element with a full-wave solver. That's Episode 9.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:26.57,0:04:29.77,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}On a computer, a flat lens takes two steps:
Dialogue: 0,0:04:29.81,0:04:33.15,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}multiply the field by e^(ik₀(n − 1)h),
Dialogue: 0,0:04:33.19,0:04:38.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}then propagate it with the angular spectrum method from Episode 1.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:38.25,0:04:40.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Three pitfalls: sample the finest zone
Dialogue: 0,0:04:40.82,0:04:42.83,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}with several points, wrap the heights,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:42.87,0:04:46.62,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and simulate the profile you can really make, not the drawing.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:46.69,0:04:48.86,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Blur this profile by a quarter of a micron,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:48.90,0:04:51.66,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and a quarter of the focused light is gone.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:52.48,0:04:55.95,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}A thin element only delays light. Phase wraps at 2π,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:55.99,0:05:00.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}so a lens can be a micron thick, until “thin” stops being true.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:00.47,0:05:02.12,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The price: steps scatter light,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:02.16,0:05:05.66,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}colours split apart, and fine zones stop being thin.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:05.73,0:05:08.86,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}And a lens's outer zones look a lot like a grating.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:08.90,0:05:12.45,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}So next time: what does periodic structure do to light?
